      <h2 id="step-2-the-tag" class="group scroll-mt-20">Step 2: The tag<a href="#step-2-the-tag" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h2>
      <Steps>
      <Step>
      <strong>Tag the branch</strong> On the branch, once the gate is clean:
      <CodeBlock code={"git tag tutorial/final\ngit tag --points-at tutorial/25-final"} lang="bash" />
      <CodeBlock code={"tutorial/final"} lang="plaintext" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Read it</strong> A branch moves: stage 25 is rebased every time a middle stage is edited, as <a href="/dogsbay-xml-dita-tutorial-docs/reference/branches">Branches</a> describes, and its commit gets a new hash. A tag is a name for one commit. <code>tutorial/final</code> is moved by hand, to the new tip of the chain, after the ladder has been re-checked, so a script or a reader who wants the finished project can fetch that name and get a tree that passed the gate:
      <CodeBlock code={"git fetch --tags\ngit show tutorial/final:README.md | head -9"} lang="bash" />
